9 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439735} :answer-field/id #uuid "53af5bc4-d5bd-582b-8c98-b6c0fe211200" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "720a20e0-eaeb-515c-b041-a17373ebe5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39931" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{{x}^{2}\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f3c3348-eb0c-5468-aa98-ecdb90d6b160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439737} {:answer/id #uuid "1521cff8-c574-52e1-bc38-0a4fe0cf4ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 